# Build output
/bin/
/dist/
*.exe

# Go test / coverage artifacts
*.test
*.out
coverage.*

# Eval run artifacts (report.json, summary.md, failed-trial transcripts). A run
# is a point-in-time record of one endpoint's behaviour, not source.
/evals/artifacts/

# Go workspace files (local-only)
go.work
go.work.sum

# Local configuration and secrets — never commit these
.env
.env.*
*.local.yaml
*.local.yml
# ...but keep the committed templates, and ignore any real model-providers.json
# (a filled-in copy carries a live api_key) wherever it is created.
!.env.example
model-providers.json

# Tooling caches
.impeccable/

# Editors / OS
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store

# Claude Code personal settings (the shared .claude/settings.json IS committed)
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
# Parallel-session worktrees live here; without this every worktree's checkout
# shows up as untracked files in the main one. See .worktreeinclude.
.claude/worktrees/
/gate

# Terraform working directories, state and variable files. The provider plugins,
# the state and the tfvars are machine-local; .terraform.lock.hcl is NOT ignored
# — it pins provider versions and belongs in the repo, exactly like go.sum.
#
# Matched ANYWHERE in the tree, not only under deploy/gcp/*/. A path-scoped rule
# is a trap here, because what it fails to match is a file that routinely holds
# credentials in plaintext: a `terraform` command run from the repository root —
# by a person, a reviewer, or a tool — writes ./terraform.tfstate, which the old
# rule did not cover, and one was staged for commit before this was widened. An
# empty state is harmless; the next one would not have been.
**/.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
tfplan
**/tfplan
# A generated Helm values file, in case one is written here anyway — the README
# says to keep it outside the tree precisely because credentials get appended to it.
deploy/gcp/**/values*.yaml
